{
  "page": 37,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein",
      "bbox": [
        170.1251983642578,
        39.52751922607422,
        407.9410095214844,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "305",
      "bbox": [
        428.84710693359375,
        39.52751922607422,
        443.8030700683594,
        47.82143783569336
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, Chopin (2007) (see also Fearnhead and Liu (2007)) formulated change-point models as state",
      "bbox": [
        57.05621337890625,
        57.378700256347656,
        443.6039733886719,
        65.6905517578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "space models, where the state xt =.θt, dt/ comprises the current parameter θt and the time since last change",
      "bbox": [
        48.089813232421875,
        66.58805084228516,
        443.6015930175781,
        76.99810791015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "dt. Then we may use sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) methods to recover the trajectory x1:T , i.e. all the",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        77.30387115478516,
        443.6016540527344,
        86.960693359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "change dates and parameter values. It works well when xt forgets its past sufﬁciently quickly, but this",
      "bbox": [
        48.08837890625,
        87.2664566040039,
        443.6056823730469,
        96.92327880859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "forbids hierarchical priors for the durations and the parameters. PHM removes this limitation: Chopin’s",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        97.2291030883789,
        443.60369873046875,
        105.54095458984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(2007) SMC algorithm may be embedded in a PHM algorithm, where each iteration corresponds to differ-",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        107.19168853759766,
        443.608154296875,
        115.5035400390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ent hyperparameters. This comes at a cost, however, as each Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) iteration",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        117.1542739868164,
        443.60107421875,
        125.46612548828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "runs a complete SMC algorithm.",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        127.11685943603516,
        173.06239318847656,
        135.4287109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Regarding (b), several questions, which have already been answered in the standard SMC case, may be",
      "bbox": [
        57.055084228515625,
        137.07945251464844,
        443.61004638671875,
        145.39129638671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "asked again for particle MCMC methods. Does residual resampling outperform multinomial resampling?",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        147.0420379638672,
        443.6081848144531,
        155.3538818359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Is the algorithm with N +1 particles strictly better than that with N particles? What happens about Rao–",
      "bbox": [
        48.08868408203125,
        156.25143432617188,
        443.60382080078125,
        165.31646728515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blackwellization, or the choice of the proposal distribution? One technical difﬁculty is that marginalizing",
      "bbox": [
        48.087799072265625,
        166.9672088623047,
        443.603759765625,
        175.279052734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "out components always reduces the variance in SMC sampling, but not in MCMC sampling. Another",
      "bbox": [
        48.087799072265625,
        176.92979431152344,
        443.60113525390625,
        185.24163818359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "difﬁculty is that particle MCMC methods retains only one particle trajectory x1:T ; hence the effect of",
      "bbox": [
        48.087799072265625,
        186.89234924316406,
        443.6015319824219,
        196.5496826171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reducing variability between particles is less obvious.",
      "bbox": [
        48.089111328125,
        196.8555145263672,
        246.9458770751953,
        205.1673583984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, obtaining a single trajectory x1:T from a forward ﬁlter is certainly much easier than obtaining",
      "bbox": [
        57.055511474609375,
        206.8180694580078,
        443.60186767578125,
        216.47500610351562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "many of them, but it may still be demanding in some scenarios, i.e. there may be so much degeneracy in",
      "bbox": [
        48.0894775390625,
        216.7808074951172,
        443.6127014160156,
        225.0926513671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "x1 that not even one particle contains an x1 in the support of p.x1|y1:T /.",
      "bbox": [
        48.0894775390625,
        225.99044799804688,
        317.9428405761719,
        236.40029907226562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Rong Chen (Rutgers University, Piscataway)",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        246.17112731933594,
        213.93798828125,
        254.48297119140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It is a pleasure to congratulate the authors on an impressive, timely and important paper. The problem of",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        256.1336669921875,
        443.5998229980469,
        264.4455261230469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "parameter estimation for complex dynamic systems by using sequential Monte Carlo methods has been",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        266.0962219238281,
        443.6042785644531,
        274.4080810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "known as a very difﬁcult problem. The authors provide a clean and powerful way to deal with such a",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        276.05877685546875,
        443.6097412109375,
        284.3706359863281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "problem. The method will certainly become a popular and powerful tool for solving complex problems.",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        286.0213317871094,
        438.66558837890625,
        294.33319091796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "I wish to concentrate my discussion on one aspect—the resampling scheme. The current paper seems",
      "bbox": [
        57.055572509765625,
        295.98388671875,
        443.6068420410156,
        304.2957458496094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to insist on resampling by using the current weights (e.g. assumption 2). We note that the procedure",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        305.9464416503906,
        443.6131286621094,
        314.25830078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "proposed actually works for more ﬂexible resampling schemes. In a way, we can view that a ﬂexible",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        315.90899658203125,
        443.60784912109375,
        324.2208557128906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "resampling scheme is in effect changing the intermediate distributions. More speciﬁcally, in the nota-",
      "bbox": [
        48.08917236328125,
        325.8715515136719,
        443.6025695800781,
        334.18341064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "tion of the paper, a ﬂexible resampling scheme operates as follows. At times n=2, . . . , T, ﬁrst construct",
